#! The `byte` type in the one surface an editor user reads it from (le07).
#!
#! This is the ONLY v0.2.4 change that reaches this repository's wire.
#! wolf-lang s136 (#231, D72) moved `str.bytes()` and the eight byte
#! producing/consuming builtins from `List[int]` to `List[byte]`
#! ([type.byte]), and hover is where a type becomes prose a human sees.
#! Every other transcript in this library re-recorded across
#! `v0.2.3..v0.2.4` with a header-only diff, which is exactly why this
#! one had to be written: a header-only sweep proves the wire did not
#! move, and proves NOTHING about a type the library never bound.
#!
#! Five positions, one per shape the byte tier takes:
#!   25:8   `let bs = s.bytes()`      — the changed binding. `List[byte]`
#!                                      here, `List[int]` at every pin
#!                                      through v0.2.3.
#!   17:8   `for b in bs`             — a BARE `byte`. The element type
#!                                      exists on its own, not only under
#!                                      a `List`.
#!   15:7   `fn dup(bs: List[byte])`  — a parameter, i.e. the type as
#!                                      WRITTEN rather than as inferred;
#!                                      both must print the same string.
#!   32:8   `let lead = bs[5] as int` — [type.byte.cast]'s widening seen
#!                                      from the outside: `int`, not
#!                                      `byte`, because the cast is where
#!                                      the octet leaves its domain.
#!   15:16  the type NAME `byte`      — hover on the builtin type name
#!                                      itself, which answers `null`.
#!                                      Recorded on purpose: `byte` is a
#!                                      builtin type NAME and not a
#!                                      keyword ([type.byte] says so, and
#!                                      `[gram.inv.kw]` stays at 50), and
#!                                      a reader is entitled to know that
#!                                      the server has nothing to say
#!                                      about it. A future pin that
#!                                      documents builtins flips this
#!                                      record, which is the point.
name      requests/hover-byte
profile   minimal

initialize
open      strings/bytes_roundtrip.lu
wait      diagnostics strings/bytes_roundtrip.lu
req       hover strings/bytes_roundtrip.lu 25:8
req       hover strings/bytes_roundtrip.lu 17:8
req       hover strings/bytes_roundtrip.lu 15:7
req       hover strings/bytes_roundtrip.lu 32:8
req       hover strings/bytes_roundtrip.lu 15:16
shutdown
